Z||000000||ineedafiero||08-24-2001||02:27 PM||||i want a fiero gt and i just found out some info thats kinda discouragin.is this whole bad wiring thing true and if so what are the odds my fiero catching a blaze??.and second has anybody ever owned a fiero threw a winter cause i need a economical car and i want to be able to get around the winter i dont think the TA will make it at all.oh yeah and lastly are the motors really in the back?try not to laugh at my stpuid ?'s ive never even sat in one b4 but i love the GTs so much i have to have one.||||reg||5
Z||000001||falconhulk||08-24-2001||02:45 PM||||The fires only occured in a few of the 84 fiero and all the recall work was done so that risk does not exist now.  To my knowledge there were no fires from bad wiring.  Look at the age of these cars on this board and they are still around!  That should tell you something<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||08-24-2001||02:47 PM||||First, The engine is where the backseat would be on a regular car. So there is still a trunk behind the engine.<BR>It's a mid-engined car. The MR2 and the old Porsche 914's are also mid-engined. <BR> Second only a small percentage of 1984 4-cyl Fiero's were the "fire" problem engines. (Note: all GM 2.5L engines that were used in MANY other GM platforms had the same problem) The fires were caused by mechanical/design problems of the actual engine. The wiring is fine. All Fiero GT's have V-6's and do not catch fire.<BR>As for winter driving the Fiero's hold there own. With the engine being over the drive tires, traction is better than any front engine/rear drive car. The only problem I've ever had was in very deep snow (8-10 inches deep) it's hard to drive with snow flying up the hood.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>My suggestion is find one in good shape and take it for a spin. Any problems with it, we've all been there and can guide you along. <P>BTW ~ Welcome!<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||08-24-2001||02:49 PM|||| oops double post  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||08-24-2001||02:49 PM||||Don't believe the negative hoopla and hype - they're *great* cars. Some of the early '84's had fire problems from the fuel line being routed across the top of the engine, but ever since then the fuel lines have been rerouted safely.<P>Buy one, and drive the heck out of it. Had'em for 11 years and over a quarter million miles - I will never be without a Fiero if I can help it.<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||08-24-2001||02:54 PM||||The engine is in back of the seats. It is a mid engine configuration.  The engine is in front of the rear wheels.  There were no unusual wiring problems with the Fiero GT that caused fires. (I don't know where you got that bogus information)<P>Many people do drive their Fieros in the winter (I'm not one)and if you are easy with the gas pedal, with the engine in front of the rear drive wheels, you can get good traction with a Fiero as a winter car.  Gas milage will vary depending on engine-transmission condition and personal driving habits.  The 2.8 V6 should get in the low 20 mpg-30 mpg.  Remember the newest Fiero is 13 years old and that would be an 88.  A 1988 Fiero GT is the most desired, with its updated suspension and may be more expensive that an 86-87 GT to purchase and to repair. (especially suspension components)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fleabitdog||08-24-2001||02:56 PM||||Welcome to the forum. Fiero's are addicting. I am on my fourth GT now (1-86, 3-88's) The worst prolems I have had were two water pumps, two alternators and about 4 headlight motors. Yes the engines are in the back. You have found a very good site( I beleive it is the best) for Fiero info and lotsa of great Fiero people. I remember my first GT. I saw it at a car lot and I had always loved the styling. I kep't thinking of the stories I had heard and after about an hour of looking at other cars ( I kep't coming back to the Fiero to look at it again) Finally I said screw it. I always wanted one so I took the chance. I have never regretted it. My first GT i owned for four years and replaced the wpump,alt, and a head light motor. I think tha is pretty darn good. If I had the money and the space I would buy every nice Fiero I saw. Sorry so long a post. Dave||||reg||14
Z||000007||FieroLisa||08-24-2001||03:27 PM||||I don't know exactly where this bad wiring thang got started, but someone tried to tell me that my GT would burn up because of it.  <BR>There's no saying any car can't catch fire. I saw a mid 90's taurus wagon burn up this spring... but as for the "initial" cause that made for all the bad publicity, if the car has gone for 15 or more years, I'm pretty sure it'll keep goin!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||08-24-2001||03:31 PM||||The whole fire thing is just a bunch of BS... I've only seen one that actually caught fire (bought it for $20 and took the only remaining parts: lower trim pieces- now on my GT).  Anyone who says they start fires hasn't done any research and doesn't really know anything about the cars!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierofire1.jpg"> <BR>I know I shouldn't show these types of stereotypical photos, but I just can't help myself!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
