Z||000012||Steve Normington||08-24-2001||04:18 PM||||"You can't handle the truth!"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"><P>Note: GT is not a body style, but a model. If what you want is the Fiero where the roofline slopes back almost to the trunk, that is called a fastback. The 85 GT's (and early 86's?) did not have this style, they had the notchback. This is where the roofline drops off in front of the rear wheels.<P>PS. The one in falconhulk's post is a notchback, the one in MintGreenGT's post (not the burned one) is a fastback.||||reg||1
Z||000013||MinnGreenGT||08-24-2001||04:18 PM||||Anybody notice the damaged silver coupe in the background?  I've got pics of my parent's yard that look like a Fiero graveyard.  My Dad finally sold all the partial cars & parts cars to Darrell Morse a couple of months ago!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That is all except my '84 SE... anybody want it?<P>{EDIT} I believe there were no true 86 GT's only 85GT notchback, and 86 1/2 Fastback- it was released midyear.<BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000014||ineedafiero||08-24-2001||05:42 PM||||wow u guys have been a huge help u all have put my "buring" desire to having a second thought about buying one.so basically what your all saying is stay away from 1984s thats fine i want something somewhat new like a 87 or 88 and im a bit confused about the whole 86 gt thing..so what your trin to say is that theres no such thing as a 86 gt it was called a fastback instead???????i might be looking at a gt im not sure what year but its 5 speed is there anything i should pay special attention to when i go to look at it again??????||||reg||7
Z||000015||mbramble||08-24-2001||06:30 PM||||There were GT's in every year except 84.  In 85 they were notchback.  The GT model was not available until the middle of the 86 model year and when it did become available it was the newly designed fastback.  So the 85GT was a notchback and 86-88 were fastbacks.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Old Lar||08-24-2001||06:41 PM||||1986 was the first year with the "fastback" design.  The early 86 GT models had the fastbact with a four speed manual transmission or 3 speed automatic.  By mid 1986 the manual transmission for the GT model was a 5 speed getrag transmission.  The 87 and 88 GT models came with a 5 speed manual or 3 speed automatic transmission.  The 86 GTs and 87 GTs are nearly identical with some different paint options available for 1987 notable a bright blue and med red metallic, and an engraved Fiero intake manifold for 87.<P>88s had a better suspension and monochromatic paint scheme with a yellow offered for the only time.  All GTs had HO V6 engines.<P>If you have doubts about the Fiero, buy a riced out Honda||||reg||1
