Z||000021||Mach10||08-25-2001||02:35 PM||||Fieros catch fire. So do other cars. You put a flammable liquid near a big heat source, with lots of flammable bits. In the past few weeks I've seen an old dodge burn to the pavement, and Eagle Vista. Hmm. I haven't seen a sinlge burnt-out Fiero. Go to the insurance lots, and count the number of Geos, Dodge Spirits, Mustangs, and Volkswagens that have caught fire, then compare to the number of Fieros in the lot.<P>The "Bad Wiring" problem you describe is when sometimes some retaining clips would break, and the wiring would move too close to the exhaust manifolds. Everything would short out, and there was a POSSIBILITY of a fire. Usually it just stunk up the car, and the engine would die. No biggie||||reg||8
Z||000022||dlancer407||08-25-2001||03:20 PM||||I would think that a Fiero would do fairly well in the snow with the engine in the middle. Heavy snow might be a problem since the car sits so low. One other thing you might want to check not mentioned above is to check any Fiero for rust in the trunk area, and around the rear wheel wells. Even though the body is plastic, the space frame and engine cradle are metal.||||reg||6
